package "main"

import "pkg/op"

// Native AArch64 backend bug: cross-package by-value pass of a
// managed-aware struct that doesn't fully fit in the remaining arg
// registers gets ABI-mismatched between caller and callee.
//
// Repro shape:
//   - 56-byte (3 ints + @[]char) Operand-style struct in pkg/op
//   - Encode(cond int, rt int, op Operand): leading 2 ints leave only
//     6 reg slots (X2..X7) but op needs 7 words
//   - Conformance compiles main.bn with -backend native, and pkg/op
//     with the LLVM backend (per cmd/bnc/main.bn:194 — deps still go
//     through LLVM until the native backend self-hosts the runtime).
//   - LLVM's pkg/op.Encode prologue reads op as 6 regs + 1 stack arg
//     (the per-element split convention LLVM emits for byval structs).
//     Native main's emitCall puts the WHOLE 7-word op on stack —
//     CallArgRegStart returns -1 once ngrn+w > 8, so the caller-side
//     code never fills X2..X7.
//   - Result: Encode receives op[0]..op[5] from whatever leftover
//     values are in X2..X7 at the call (i.e., garbage), and reads
//     op.Kind (= X2's leftover value) from a stale spill rather than
//     the value main set.
//
// Same shape but with the struct entirely fitting in remaining regs
// (e.g. 2 ints + @[]char = 5 words after 2 leading ints = 7 regs ≤
// 8) does NOT reproduce — both backends agree on the all-in-regs
// case. Pure-int structs of the same total size also pass — the bug
// requires the managed-slice field, presumably because LLVM's ABI
// emission differs once the struct contains a managed pointer.
//
// Fix: native CallArgRegStart / CallArgStackOff and emitCall +
// prologue need to support split passing (fill remaining regs, put
// overflow on stack) to match LLVM's behavior.

func main() {
	var o op.Operand
	o.Kind = op.OP_MEM_IMM
	o.A = 1
	o.B = 2
	var r int = op.Encode(14, 0, o)
	if r != 1 {
		print("FAIL Encode saw wrong Kind, returned ")
		println(r)
		return
	}
	println("PASS")
}
